<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hermann Billung</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/973">973</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hermann Billung was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> noble whom Otto I entrusted around 936 with command against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> along the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>, the frontier later called the Billung March. He governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> for Otto during the king's absences and is counted as the first Billung duke of Saxony. His family held the ducal title until 1106.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hermann-billung.jpg" alt="Hermann Billung" width="320" height="441" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lucas Cranach the Younger · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hermann_Billung.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hermann was born in 900 or 912, probably a son of Billung, and was the younger brother of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> count Wichmann the Elder. His rise owed much to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottonian-dynasty">Ottonian dynasty</a>, Saxon dukes who had become kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> with the accession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler">Henry the Fowler</a> in 919 and now had to look to the affairs of the whole realm. When Otto I came to the throne in 936 he made Hermann a military commander, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princeps">princeps</a> militiae, over the Saxon lands north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>, passing over the claims of Wichmann, a brother-in-law of Queen Dowager Matilda. Wichmann joined the unsuccessful rebellion of Otto's half-brother Thankmar and Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eberhard-of-franconia">Eberhard of Franconia</a> in 938.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The frontier command reached from the Limes Saxoniae along the Baltic coast towards the Peene River, roughly the later Mecklenburg. With more autonomy than the neighbouring margrave Gero, Hermann exacted tribute from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polabian-slavs">Polabian Slavs</a> of the Obotrite federation. On his brother's death in 944 he also became count in the Bardengau around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a>, where he founded the monastery of St Michael, again passing over his nephews Wichmann the Younger and Egbert the One-Eyed. Both joined the rebellion of Duke Liudolf of Swabia in 953, and during that crisis Otto, himself <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-saxony">Duke of Saxony</a>, entrusted more and more of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> authority to Hermann in his absences. In 961, when Otto marched into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, he made Hermann his administrator, procurator regis, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Hermann's exact position is unclear: royal documents never call him dux, only military leader, count and margrave, and historians have long debated how far he held a duchy or an organised march. In 972 Archbishop Adalbert of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> received him like a king, which annoyed the emperor. Hermann died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quedlinburg">Quedlinburg</a> on 27 March 973. His son Bernard inherited and strengthened his position and was recognised as duke. Through his daughters Mathilde, married first to Count Baldwin III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, and Suanhilde, married to two margraves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a>, the family was tied to other leading houses.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hermann Billung” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Otto I the Saxon frontier became a system of marches: <b>Hermann Billung</b> commanded on the lower Elbe and Margrave Gero in the middle Elbe lands, imposing tribute on the Slavs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-saxony">Duchy of Saxony · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 936 Otto I entrusted <b>Hermann Billung</b> with command against the Slavs along the lower Elbe, from Holstein to the Peene.</blockquote><a href="/state/billung-march">Billung March</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quedlinburg">Quedlinburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.79,11.15&amp;year=963" aria-label="Quedlinburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Hermann Billung"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5586 5650h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5586 5650h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=51.79,11.15&amp;year=963">What was here in 963 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
